From tru64-unix-managers  Mon Jun 07 14:30:49 1999
From: bantolovich () specialmetals ! com
Date: Mon, 07 Jun 1999 14:30:49 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: [SUMMARY]Forcing a 24bit depth for Powerstorm 4D10T (ELSA GLORIA)
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96079044115580



Thanks to all those who responded and helped me get my system running
correctly.

The original problem concerned switching the default number of planes from
16 to 24 on an 8MB video card. The solution was to modify the
/etc/dt/config/Xservers file beyond specifying TrueColor. The following
lines as suggested by Arnold Sutter and  Graham Allan respectively both
seemed to work quite well.


   :0 Local local@console /usr/bin/X11/X :0 -su -bs -vclass0 TrueColor
-screen0 1280 -depth 24 -terminate

   :0   Local local@console /usr/bin/X11/X :0 -bs -su -screen 1280x1024
-vclass TrueColor -depth 24

Grapham also said "they suggest copying /var/dt/Xservers to
/etc/dt/config/Xservers and
modifying that, as the original copy may be replaced during OS upgrades."

Thanks again for all the help.
Bruce



ORIGINAL MESSAGE
================
I recently installed an ELSA GLORIA synergy video card on a digital unix
box (PC-164LX motherboard) running DU4.0D. This was a video card suggested
by the digital web site along with the specification that OPEN3d4.92 is
necessary. Everything installed well and the card defaulted to high res and
8plane graphics mode. I tried to force a 24 plane mode by modifying the
/var/dt/Xservers file to include the options -vclass0 TrueColor.
Unfortunately, this doesn't seem to force a 24plane mode but instead a
16plane mode. [Normally this wouldn't be a problem but I am also using Pro
Engineer which thinks that is should have 24 planes (probably due to the
TrueColor specificaiton) and has big colormapping problems when it only has
16 planes.] Based on this, I really think that I need to force the 24 plane
mode but am unsure why it isn't already being enforced. The graphics card
has 8MB and should be able to go to 1280X1024X24planes according to product
literature and the open3d install manual which states that the elsa gloria
and powerstorm 4d10t should be treated as if they are the same.

Does anyone have any suggestions about how to set the 24plane mode.

Thanks in advance,
Bruce

From tru64-unix-managers  Mon Jun 07 13:51:13 1999
From: "Leonard, Roger" <rleonard () cvty ! com>
Date: Mon, 07 Jun 1999 13:51:13 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: SUMMARY: defragment cron job
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96079044115579

Original Posting:


> We do oracle exports during the night and have had errors and I am looking
>real hard at the defragment job.  Does anyone have any words of wisdom on
>the defragment job that runs by default in roots cron?  The default period
>is from 0100 to 0500.  Can I make it run faster by using the -T option or
-t
>option, or does that just stop it before the defrag is complete?  The man
>page is not real clear on that. Does anyone not use it and why?  Has anyone
>seen problems with it during oracle exports? Has anyone modified the
>defaults?  Any help is appreciated and I will summarize.


Summary:
Many thanks to all who replied.  Responses varied from "I run it only when
no other activity is present"  to "If you value your data don't run
defragment"  Oy.  Bottom line is I commented out the cron job and replaced
it with one that uses the -vn argument and mails me the results.  This will
merely look at the ADVFS stats and let you know when your fs is getting
fragged.   Then I will schedule a time to stop all system activity  (I'm
allotted 2 seconds a month) and backup and defrag.  The pertinent data is
Aggregate I/O perf and Avg exts per file w/exts.  The best case is 100% for
the first and 1.00 for the last.  Special thanks to Bryan Lavelle and Tom
Blinn who took the time to respond to a couple of additional questions and
clarification of the ADVFS.  Here is a sample of my defrag after 2 days of
not defragging:

> defragment -vn domain02
defragment: Gathering data for domain 'domain02'
  Current domain data:
    Extents:                   7075
    Files w/extents:           6851
    Avg exts per file w/exts:  1.03
    Aggregate I/O perf:        100%
    Free space fragments:       146
                     <100K     <1M    <10M    >10M
      Free space:      0%      0%      1%     98%
      Fragments:       41      70      15      20

By the way my exports and backups now take half the time.  The defrag
process must be a real hog of resources.

Roger D. Leonard
